    {
      "text": "3.2 Accurate Inflating Hypercubes via Principal Component Analysis",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003051757812,
        545.7216796875,
        471.03314208984375,
        555.3653564453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a mathematical technique used to identify the",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        559.3726806640625,
        521.8499145507812,
        569.0054321289062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "principal directions of variation in a dataset. Given a dataset of L data points, xi ∈Rn, i ∈[L],",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        570.94873046875,
        523.503662109375,
        584.19140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PCA estimates the covariance matrix, Σ ⪰0, Σ ∈Rn×n of data points xi, i ∈[L]. The",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        582.5126342773438,
        521.9965209960938,
        597.1702270507812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "eigenvectors of Σ, known as principal components, define the directions along which",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        599.966064453125,
        521.9439697265625,
        609.6533813476562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the data exhibits the highest variance, while the corresponding eigenvalues quantify the",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        613.5696411132812,
        521.8611450195312,
        623.202392578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "magnitude of variance along each direction. These principal components form an orthonormal",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        627.11865234375,
        521.8382568359375,
        636.7514038085938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "basis that aligns with the natural structure of the data, providing key insights into its intrinsic",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        640.6676635742188,
        521.8489379882812,
        650.3004150390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "geometric properties.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        654.2176513671875,
        189.77462768554688,
        663.8504028320312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "4. However, if the set of initial states I is large and the partitioning of I is not scalable (high dimensional",
      "bbox": [
        93.68299865722656,
        674.2191772460938,
        521.9969482421875,
        682.6834716796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "states), we remain limited to using approx star. Nevertheless, even for this case, the small size of the",
      "bbox": [
        104.94400024414062,
        685.7251586914062,
        521.9800415039062,
        693.6425170898438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "model significantly reduces the conservatism of approx star.",
      "bbox": [
        104.94400024414062,
        696.6841430664062,
        346.1311340332031,
        704.6015014648438
      ],
